  nonce: f993  # f993
  data:  # f993
    kind: work_item  # f993
    title: "The python analyzer pass is 87.5% of analysis time and its PER-FILE cost is 7.1x higher on pretix than kserve at 1.17x the node yield — possible superlinearity in project size"  # f993
    status: todo_soft  # f993
    priority: 3  # f993
    parent:  # f993
    tags:  # f993
    - performance  # f993
    - analysis_quality  # f993
    - measurement  # f993
    isbefore: []  # f993
    duplicate_of: []  # f993
    not_duplicate_of: []  # f993
    pr_ref:  # f993
    description: "FOUND 2026-09-08 while answering \"why does pretix take so long\", and MEASURED\nrather than reasoned. Artifacts: `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/py_scaling_09092026/`\n(PLAN.md pre-registered, kserve.survey.json, curve.sh).\n\nTHE PYTHON ANALYZER PASS IS THE COST, and it is not close. From pretix's\n`analysis_runs` (90 passes):\n\n    python       1692.3 s   87.5%   1199 files\n    javascript    152.3 s    7.9%    180 files\n    html           28.5 s    1.5%    459 files\n    ~60 linkers    ~40   s   ~2%\n\nThe Django-specific linkers this was first attributed to -- django_orm_dispatch\nand friends, over a class-method graph 0016 measured at 896 dispatch pairs and\n16,010 contains pairs -- cost about **5 seconds**. That hypothesis is REFUTED.\n\nIT IS NOT REPO SIZE. pretix is 119 MB and takes 2745 s end to end; kserve is\n199 MB and takes 175 s. The BIGGER repository runs 16x faster. (This is the\nstanding \"never extrapolate cost from repo size\" rule producing another\ninstance.)\n\nIT IS NOT PER-FILE CONTENT DENSITY EITHER, which is the part that makes this a\npossible defect rather than a fact about Django. Both numbers now MEASURED from\n`analysis_runs`, same instrument, cold cache:\n\n    repo     python pass   files   s/file   nodes/file\n    pretix     1692.3 s     1199    1.411      17.8\n    kserve      107.1 s      542    0.198      15.2\n\n**7.1x the per-file cost for 1.17x the per-file node yield, at 2.2x the file\ncount.** If per-file cost scaled as N^k, these two points give k ~= 2.5, i.e.\nTOTAL cost ~ N^3.5 -- but two points across two repositories cannot separate\nproject SIZE from code STYLE, so that exponent is an arithmetic consequence of\nthe pair, NOT a measurement.\n\nWHAT IS RUNNING NOW, and why the design is this one: a WITHIN-REPO curve on\npretix at `--max-files` 150 / 300 / 600 / 1200, same tree, cold cache each,\nwhich controls for style completely. Its confound is that `--max-files`\ntruncates to an ORDERED PREFIX (the standing rule: never extrapolate from an\nordered prefix), so the pre-registered CONTROL is to report nodes/file and\nedges/file at every N beside s/file. s/file rising while nodes/file stays flat\nleaves scaling in N as the surviving explanation; the two rising together means\nthe prefix is unrepresentative and the design failed.\n\nPRE-REGISTERED READINGS. SUPERLINEAR = s/file rises >1.5x from N=150 to N=1200\nwith nodes/file flat. LINEAR = s/file roughly constant, in which case\npretix-vs-kserve is a CONTENT difference, there is no scaling defect, and this\nitem closes as a measured \"not a defect\". INCONCLUSIVE = nodes/file moves with\ns/file, needing a random-sample subsetter instead of a prefix.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS EVEN IF THE ABSOLUTE COST IS TOLERABLE. DEEP mode exists to test\n20-200 MB repositories, and a measurement arm on pretix costs ~35-46 minutes per\narm -- 0020 spent about three hours on four arms, and the 8-hour experiment\nceiling is reached by roughly a six-repository two-arm design. If per-file cost\ngrows with project size, the ceiling binds on repository COUNT much sooner than\na size-based estimate predicts, which is exactly the extrapolation error that\ncost measurement 0019 its first cohort.\n\nNOT MEASURED: which part of the python analyzer scales. This measures the PASS,\nnot its internals; a profile is the next instrument if the reading is\nSUPERLINEAR. Nothing here covers other languages -- go on kserve is 0.086\ns/file, but that is one repository and not the subject."  # f993
    fields: {}  # f993
- op: discuss  # 8428
  at: '2026-09-09T01:54:16Z'  # 8428
  by: agent  # 8428
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8428
  clock: 2  # 8428
  nonce: '8428'  # 8428
  message: "CURVE RUN, READING IS **SUPERLINEAR**, and the confound is CONTROLLED rather\nthan acknowledged. 2026-09-09, tree 3ba0fda085, full record\n`~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/py_scaling_09092026/RESULT.md`.\n\n`hypergumbo survey <pretix> --max-files N`, cold cache per run, sequential\n(timings cannot overlap). Python pass from `analysis_runs`:\n\n    N     files   py sec   s/file   nodes/f   edges/f\n    150     150     63.3    0.422     23.6      84.9\n    300     300     74.7    0.249     13.5      46.2\n    600     600    287.0    0.478     15.0      56.8\n   1200    1199   1700.0    1.418     17.8      94.3\n\nTHE ORDERED-PREFIX CONFOUND IS REFUTED BY THE DATA. The pre-registered control\nwas nodes/file and edges/file beside s/file, and they move OPPOSITE to cost:\ns/file 3.36x while nodes/file **0.75x (decreased)** and edges/file 1.11x. The\nlater files are if anything sparser, so \"the prefix got harder\" does not hold.\n\nTHE CURVE IS NOT MONOTONIC AND THAT MATTERS. N=150 (0.422) sits ABOVE N=300\n(0.249) -- fixed startup cost amortised over few files. Dropping N=150 as\nfixed-cost-dominated makes the effect WORSE, not better:\n\n    N=300 -> N=1200   4x files, 5.7x per-file cost    per-file ~ N^1.26\n    N=600 -> N=1200   2x files, 2.97x per-file cost   per-file ~ N^1.57\n\n**The exponent GROWS with N.** Total python-pass cost is roughly N^2.3 to N^2.6\nover the measured range, with the trend pointing at the higher end. Total pass\nseconds 74.7 -> 1700.0 for 4x the files.\n\nAN UNPLANNED REPRODUCIBILITY CONTROL: the N=1200 pass reads 1700.0 s against\nmeasurement 0016's independent pretix survey at 1692.3 s -- different tree,\ndifferent day, 0.5% apart.\n\nSO THE TWO-REPO NUMBER IN THE DESCRIPTION IS CORROBORATED BUT IS NOT THE\nEVIDENCE. kserve's 0.198 s/file vs pretix's 1.411 is consistent with the curve;\nthe CURVE is what separates size from style, because it holds the codebase\nfixed.\n\nSTATUS: leaving `todo_soft`. This establishes THAT the pass scales superlinearly\nin file count, not WHICH part does. The next instrument is a profile at N=600\nversus N=1200 -- the two points that already differ by 2.97x per file -- which\nwould name the structure rather than the symptom. Nothing here distinguishes an\nO(N^2) project-wide structure from repeated O(N) work per file, and that\ndistinction decides whether this is fixable or inherent."  # 8428
- op: discuss  # f965
  at: '2026-09-09T01:58:55Z'  # f965
  by: agent  # f965
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f965
  clock: 3  # f965
  nonce: f965  # f965
  message: "CORRECTION TO THE PRECEDING ENTRY, same day, prompted by the owner noticing that\nedges/file \"looks suggestive\". It is, and the preceding entry's control was\ncomputed off the wrong baseline.\n\nTHE ERROR WAS A SELECTIVE BASELINE. That entry reported edges/file as \"roughly\nflat, 1.11x\" and read the prefix confound as refuted. The 1.11x is N=150 ->\nN=1200 -- and N=150 is the point the SAME entry excludes as fixed-cost-dominated\nwhen computing s/file. Using a baseline for the CONTROL that was rejected for the\nEFFECT is how a control gets made to look clean.\n\nRECOMPUTED FROM N=300, the honest baseline:\n\n    N=300 -> N=600    files 2.0x | s/file 1.92x | edges/file 1.23x | ms/edge 1.56x\n    N=300 -> N=1200   files 4.0x | s/file 5.70x | edges/file 2.04x | ms/edge 2.79x\n\n**Edges per file grows 2.04x, not 1.11x.** The finding SURVIVES and is better\nunderstood, because the cost DECOMPOSES exactly:\n\n    s/file growth = edges/file growth x ms/edge growth\n    5.70x         = 2.04x             x 2.79x            (5.69)\n\nTWO components, where the preceding entry credited all of it to the second: more\nedges are produced per file as the project grows, AND each edge costs more.\nBoth are scaling in N -- more files means more resolvable cross-file targets --\nso \"superlinear in project size\" stands. Nodes/file moves 1.32x on the same\nbaseline, well under the cost growth. The prefix is not \"harder files\"; it is\nmore INTERCONNECTED ones, which is the scaling itself.\n\nWHAT THIS CHANGES FOR THE NEXT INSTRUMENT. The preceding entry proposed\nprofiling N=600 vs N=1200. The owner proposed N=300 vs N=600 instead, and that\nis the better call: the contrast is still clean (s/file 1.92x against edges/file\n1.23x, so cost outruns edge yield) and the pair is ~6x cheaper, which is\ndecisive under cProfile where overhead would turn N=1200's 1915 s wall into\nhours. RUNNING NOW.\n\nTREE NOTE: the curve ran on `3ba0fda085` and the profile on `871fe0b204`. The\nonly commit between them is this project's own tracker auto-sync -- 4 `.ops`\nfiles, zero `.py` under `packages/` -- so the analyzer is byte-identical across\nboth and the pair is comparable."  # f965
- op: discuss  # 7345
  at: '2026-09-09T02:25:51Z'  # 7345
  by: agent  # 7345
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7345
  clock: 4  # 7345
  nonce: '7345'  # 7345
  message: "ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND QUANTIFIED, 2026-09-09, by profiling the owner's cheaper\npair (N=300 vs N=600). Full record:\n`~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/py_scaling_09092026/PROFILE-RESULT.txt`.\n\n**`symbol_resolution.py:325(_ensure_suffix_index)` is 61% of ALL the growth**:\n40.1 s -> 269.9 s tottime, 6.73x for 2x the files, 229.8 s of the 376 s total\nincrease.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, PROVEN BY CALL COUNTS RATHER THAN INFERRED:\n\n    SymbolResolver.__init__ (line 120)   5,827 -> 16,065 calls   2.76x\n    lookup_symbol           (line 885)   5,826 -> 16,064 calls   2.76x\n    _ensure_suffix_index    (line 325)  12,560 -> 29,033 calls   2.31x\n    _ensure_suffix_index    tottime       40.1s ->  269.9s       6.73x\n\n`__init__` and `lookup_symbol` track each other TO WITHIN ONE CALL -- and that\none is py.py:8306's single legitimate resolver. So **every `lookup_symbol` call\nconstructs a fresh `SymbolResolver`, whose `_ensure_suffix_index` then rebuilds\nthe ENTIRE suffix index over the whole registry** (for each of R registry keys,\nsplit the module and join every one of its D suffixes). The corroborating inner\nloop is visible too: `str.join` calls 89M -> 570M (6.37x) and `str.split`\n24.5M -> 151M (6.18x).\n\nTHE DECOMPOSITION IS EXACT: 2.76x more rebuilds x 2.4x cost per rebuild\n(6.9 ms -> 16.8 ms as the registry grows) = 6.7x. That IS the quadratic:\nO(lookups x registry), and both factors grow with file count.\n\nBLAST RADIUS: `py.py:440(_lookup_symbol_by_module)` carries **680.4 s cumulative\nof the 936 s profiled run -- 73% of the whole survey.**\n\nHOW THE FAST PATH IS MISSED. `_lookup_symbol_by_module` takes an OPTIONAL\n`resolver` and falls back at py.py:471 to the one-off `lookup_symbol` when it is\nNone. All nine call sites in py.py syntactically pass `resolver=`, but SIX\nfunctions in the chain declare `resolver: SymbolResolver | None = None` and\nforward it (py.py lines 395, 445, 2408, 4567, 6275, 7145), so any caller that\ndoes not thread one silently drops to the quadratic path. At N=600, 16,064 of\nthe 35,173 `_lookup_symbol_by_module` calls -- **46%** -- arrive with None.\n\nTHE DESIGN ALREADY WARNED ABOUT THIS, IN TWO DOCSTRINGS. `lookup_symbol`: \"For\nrepeated lookups, prefer creating a SymbolResolver instance to benefit from index\ncaching.\" `_lookup_symbol_by_module`: \"For repeated lookups, pass a pre-built\nresolver for better performance (cached indexes).\" An optional parameter whose\nomission is a silent 6.7x is a footgun, not a preference -- and this is a\nmeasured instance of the general shape.\n\nFIX DIRECTIONS, in preference order:\n  (a) CACHE THE INDEX ON THE REGISTRY, not the resolver -- key it on registry\n      identity so a fresh resolver over the same registry reuses the built\n      index. Makes the fast path the DEFAULT and cannot be missed by a caller.\n  (b) Thread the resolver through the six forwarding functions. Fixes today's\n      instance; leaves the footgun for the next caller.\n  (a) is preferred on exactly the reasoning that produced this defect.\n\nSTILL NOT ESTABLISHED: whether the other analyzers that use these resolvers\n(r_lang, starlark, csharp, dart, framework_patterns) hit the same path -- the\nprofile is a PYTHON-pass profile on one repository. The `_index_key_suffixes` /\nNameResolver variants at lines 486/606/628 are near-free here (0.0 s), so the\ndefect as measured is specific to `SymbolResolver`'s suffix index."  # 7345
